tel 1.0

base sigillate
import soundness

info   JOSE: signed and encrypted tokens, keys as JSON, and bearer authentication
detail
    _Sigillate_ implements [JOSE](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7515), the standards
    (RFC 7515–7519) behind the tokens web APIs authenticate with: `Jws`, content with a
    signature or MAC over it; `Jwe`, encrypted content; `Jwk` and `Jwks`, keys as JSON; and
    `Jwt`, a set of claims carried in either. They are built over enigmatic's keys and ciphers
    and jacinta's JSON.

    The key's type chooses the algorithm. A header's `alg` is compared with the algorithm
    the key's cipher stands for, and is never used to decide what to do with the key, so a
    token cannot be relabelled to be checked some other way:

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.textDecodable
    import codepages.utf8Codepage, charsets.utf8Charset, textSanitizers.skipSanitizer
    import cloaks.heapCloak
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import providers.javaBaseProvider
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

    val key = PrivateKey.generate[Ecdsa[256]]()
    val token: Text = Jwt(Jwt.Claims(issuer = t"globe", subject = t"burbage")).sign(key).in[Text]
    val jwt: Jwt = Jwt.verify(token, key.public)
    ```

    The library has three source sets:

    - `sigillate.core` — `Jws`, `Jwe`, `Jwk`, `Jwks`, `Jwt` and `Jose`, which holds the header,
      the error and the typeclasses that map a cipher to an algorithm name.
    - `sigillate.http` — `Jwks.retrieve`, a key set fetched from the URL its owner publishes
      it at.
    - `sigillate.server` — `bearerAuth`, bearer-token authentication as scintillate
      middleware, with the verified token in scope as `jwt`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. There are no choice packages of its
    own; signing and encryption need a `Crypto` provider (`providers.javaBaseProvider`),
    reading a private key needs a `Cloak` (`cloaks.heapCloak`), verifying a token needs a
    `Clock`, and every failure is a `Jose.Error`.

refer sigillate.Jws
refer sigillate.Jwe
refer sigillate.Jwk
refer sigillate.Jwt
refer sigillate.Jose.Error

entry .Jws   a JSON Web Signature: content with a signature or MAC over it and its header
  detail
      `Jws(payload, key)` signs under the algorithm the key's cipher stands for.
      `#verify` checks the signature against a public or symmetric key and gives the payload,
      which is the only way to reach it. The compact serialization is the `Text` codec; the
      JSON serializations are `#flattened`, `Jws.general` and `Jws.read`.

entry .Jwe   JSON Web Encryption: content encrypted under a key conveyed to its recipient
  detail
      `Jwe[content](payload, key)` encrypts with the content cipher named by the type
      argument, conveying its key by the key-management family the recipient's key stands
      for; `#decrypt` reverses it. Every failure to decrypt is the same error.

entry .Jwk   a JSON Web Key: a public, private or symmetric key as a JSON object
  detail
      `Jwk(key)` writes a public key, and `Jwk(key, Divulgence)` a private or symmetric one.
      `#publicKey`, `#privateKey` and `#symmetricKey` read it back as a key of a named cipher,
      and `#thumbprint` is its RFC 7638 thumbprint.

entry .Jwt   a JSON Web Token: registered claims and an application's own
  detail
      `Jwt.verify` checks a signed token against a key and its validity period against the
      `Clock` in scope. `#validate` checks its issuer and audience, and `#sign` makes a `Jws`
      of it.
